II. Gilgamesh og Enkidu mødes
II. Gilgamesh and Enkidu meet

[Den oldbabylonske version.] Mens Gilgamesh fortæller om sit syn, sidder Enkidu over for kvinden. Hun blotter sine bryster og viser ham sin skønhed, og han glemmer det sted, hvor han blev født. Seks dage og syv nætter ligger Enkidu med kvinden.
[The Old Babylonian version.] While Gilgamesh tells of his vision, Enkidu sits facing the woman. She bares her breasts and shows him her beauty, and he forgets the place where he was born. Six days and seven nights Enkidu lies with the woman.

Så tager hun ordet: »Når jeg ser på dig, Enkidu, synes jeg, du ligner en gud. Hvorfor strejfer du om på steppen med dyrene? Kom, jeg fører dig til Uruk med de brede torve, til det hellige tempel, hvor Anu bor. Kom, Enkidu, lad mig vise vej til Eanna, Anus bolig. Dér bor Gilgamesh, den ypperste af alle skabninger. Du vil tage ham i din favn som en kvinde. Du vil elske ham som dig selv. Op med dig! Hvad laver du på jorden? Det er en hyrdes seng.«
Then she speaks. “When I look at you, Enkidu, I think you resemble a god. Why roam the steppe with the animals? Come, I'll lead you to Uruk with its wide marketplaces, to the holy temple where Anu lives. Come, Enkidu, let me show the way to Eanna, Anu's dwelling. Gilgamesh lives there, the foremost of all creatures. You will take him in your arms as you would a woman. You will love him as yourself. Get up off the ground! That's a shepherd's bed.”

Han hørte hende og tog imod hendes råd. Hendes ord gik lige til hjertet på ham. Hun tog et af sine klædningsstykker af og gav ham det på. Resten beholdt hun selv. Hånd i hånd som søster og bror kom de til hyrdernes hytter [?], hvor fårene stod i fold. Da hyrderne så ham, stimlede de sammen. [Fire eller fem linjer mangler.]
He heard her and accepted her advice. Her words went straight to his heart. She took off one of her garments and dressed him in it. The rest she wore herself. Hand in hand like sister and brother they came to the shepherds' huts [?], where the sheep were penned. The shepherds gathered at the sight of him. [Four or five lines are missing.]

Han plejede at die hos de vilde dyr. Hun satte brød foran ham. Han brækkede det i stykker og sad og stirrede. Enkidu vidste ikke, hvordan man spiste brød eller drak øl. Kvinden sagde: »Smag på brødet, Enkidu. Det er det, vi lever af. Drik også af øllet. Det gør folk her i landet.« Enkidu spiste sig mæt i brød. Syv bægre øl drak han. Humøret steg, hjertet var let, og ansigtet lyste op. Han gned sig og smurte hårene på kroppen ind i olie. Sådan blev han menneske. Han tog tøj på som andre mænd, greb sit våben og jagede de løver, der om natten plagede hyrderne. Han fangede sjakalerne. Da han havde fået bugt med løverne, sov hyrderne trygt. Enkidu var deres vogter, en mand i sin fulde kraft.
He had been used to sucking milk from wild animals. She set bread before him. He broke it and sat staring. Enkidu didn't know how to eat bread or drink beer. The woman said, “Taste the bread, Enkidu. It's what we live on. Drink some beer too, as people do in this country.” Enkidu ate bread until he was full. He drank seven cupfuls of beer. His spirits rose, his heart was glad, and his face lit up. He rubbed himself down and oiled the hair on his body. So he became human. He dressed like other men, took up his weapon and hunted the lions that troubled the shepherds by night. He caught the jackals. Once he had overcome the lions, the shepherds slept soundly. Enkidu kept watch over them, a man at his full strength.

En af krigerne taler til [Gilgamesh?] […]. [Omkring tretten linjer mangler.] Midt i sin fornøjelse så Enkidu op og fik øje på en mand. »Hent ham herhen,« sagde han til kvinden. »Hvorfor er han kommet? Jeg vil vide, hvad han er ude på.« Hun kaldte manden hen til ham. »Hvad leder du efter, fremmede? Hvor skal du overnatte?«
One of the warriors speaks to [Gilgamesh?] […]. [About thirteen lines are missing.] In the midst of his enjoyment Enkidu looked up and noticed a man. “Bring him over here,” he told the woman. “Why has he come? I want to know what he's after.” She called the man over. “What are you looking for, stranger? Where are you staying?”

Manden sagde til Enkidu: »Gilgamesh kalder jer til forsamlingshuset. Det er mændenes skik og en æresbevisning til de mægtige. Kom og læg byens ofre frem. Kom med den mad, byen skal have til alles bedste. Kongen af Uruk med de brede torve skal tage imod jeres hilsen. Gilgamesh, konge af Uruk med de brede torve, skal tage imod jeres hilsen. Først ligger han med den kvinde, skæbnen har bestemt. Derefter taler han efter gudens råd og tyder sin skæbne af varslernes tegn [?].« Da de hørte det, gik de foran ham. [Omkring ni linjer mangler.]
The man said to Enkidu, “Gilgamesh calls you to the meeting house. It's the men's custom and a tribute to the powerful. Come and lay out the offerings owed to the city. Come and bring the city's food for the good of all. The king of broad-marketed Uruk is to receive your greeting. Gilgamesh, king of broad-marketed Uruk, is to receive your greeting. First he lies with the woman appointed by fate. Then he speaks on the god's advice and reads his destiny in the shape of the omens [?].” On hearing this they went ahead of him. [About nine lines are missing.]

Enkidu gik først, kvinden fulgte efter. De kom ind i Uruk med de brede torve, og folk stimlede sammen bag ham. Da han standsede på gaden, trængtes de bag ham og råbte: »Se, hvor han ligner Gilgamesh! Lidt lavere, men kraftigere bygget. […] Engang drak han mælk af vilde dyr som et hjælpeløst spædbarn. Brødene her i Uruk gør en mand fuldvoksen! Han var vild, men nu ligner han en rigtig kriger. I krop kan han måle sig med den guddommelige Gilgamesh.«
Enkidu went first and the woman followed. They entered Uruk with its broad marketplaces, and people gathered behind him. When he stopped in the street they crowded in and called out, “Look how he resembles Gilgamesh! A little shorter, but more strongly built. […] Once he drank milk from wild creatures like a helpless baby. The loaves here in Uruk bring a man to his prime! He was wild, but now he looks every inch a warrior. In build he can match godlike Gilgamesh.”

Sengen var redt til kærlighedsgudindens fest. Om natten kom Gilgamesh for at ligge med kvinden. Men ude på gaden stillede Enkidu sig i vejen for ham og truede med sin styrke […]. [Syv eller otte linjer mangler.] Gilgamesh […] bag ham […]. Vreden steg. Han fór frem, og de mødtes på gaden. Enkidu satte foden i døråbningen. Han ville ikke lade Gilgamesh komme ind. De tog livtag og prustede [?] som tyre. Tærsklen splintredes, og muren rystede. Gilgamesh og Enkidu tog livtag og prustede som tyre. Tærsklen splintredes, og muren rystede.
The bed was spread for the rites of the goddess of love. At night Gilgamesh came to lie with the woman. But there in the street Enkidu blocked his way and threatened to use his strength […]. [Seven or eight lines are missing.] Gilgamesh […] behind him […]. Anger rose. He rushed forward and they met in the street. Enkidu planted his foot in the doorway. He wouldn't let Gilgamesh in. They grappled and snorted [?] like bulls. The threshold splintered and the wall shook. Gilgamesh and Enkidu grappled and snorted like bulls. The threshold splintered and the wall shook.

Gilgamesh bøjede knæet mod jorden. Så lagde hans raseri sig, og hans kamplyst kølnedes. Da kamplysten var kølnet, sagde Enkidu til Gilgamesh: »Din mor har født én som dig, kun én. Ninsun, den bedste ko i flokken, har løftet dit hoved højt over krigerne. Enlil har givet dig kongemagten over menneskene.«
Gilgamesh bent his knee to the ground. His fury eased and his fighting spirit cooled. When it had cooled, Enkidu said to Gilgamesh, “Your mother bore one such as you, one alone. Ninsun, the finest cow in the herd, raised your head above the warriors. Enlil has given you kingship over humankind.”
